635|revfactory-harness|Revfactory Harness v2 distributes Claude Code skills whose model-guided, project-specific constructor analyses a live target project, chooses agent roles/skills/orchestration modes, and writes and validates reusable harness artifacts (S1=A). Generated workflow/persistent-team design patterns and shared tasks do not themselves establish regulation of current inter-S1 interference at the constructor recursion (S2=—); builder phase/budget/QA guidance does not form a separate discretionary current-portfolio executive (S3=—); ordinary builder verification and templates for downstream critics do not establish a complementary independent operational audit and S3 corrective-return loop (S3*=—). A separate shipped on-demand harness:evolve skill inspects user feedback, recurring operational failure and project Git/execution records, derives prospective agent/skill/orchestrator adaptation options, applies and validates semantic changes to those same reusable project files for subsequent runs (S4=A), without implying automatic continuous monitoring or parent-governed S4. Workflow consent, user-specified goals and change-reversal confirmation are task/configuration controls rather than legitimate identity or ultimate-purpose policy decision closure (S5=—). Source-bounded to the installed skill-guided model mode; no independent external Claude inference or generated target-team runtime execution is claimed.
636|pyflue|PyFlue ships a Python model-backed persistent agent session whose first-party prompt/backend wrapper supplies task context and declared tool set to packaged Pydantic AI Agent.run, returning selected tool effects and completion to the session/history (S1=A). Child tasks with isolated history, async dispatch and workflows allow delegation and transport but do not evidence interference-sensitive regulation of at least two viable S1 divisions and subsequent mutual adjustment (S2=—). Sandbox, quotas, task depth, role/provider selection and workflow admission are app-configured/current-task bounds rather than agent-owned whole-current cross-operation management rights (S3=—), while schema-repair retries, runtime traces and telemetry are not an independently mandated operational-reality audit correcting S3 (S3*=—). Skills/history compaction, backend plugins and saved sessions do not show a first-party prospective environmental option, authorized strategic adoption and return to changed future organizational capability (S4=—); operator permission and config do not establish legitimate ultimate identity/policy arbitration and returned enduring governance (S5=—). Judgment is source-bound to the packaged default Pydantic AI integration and supported sessions, not a claim of live inference or empirical throughput.
637|agentdescent|AgentDescent packages a model-backed task solver and evolving-artifact harness: LLMAgent.solve produces task-facing model responses integrated into worker rollouts/scoring (S1=A), and LLMAgent.propose/reflector uses failed task evidence to generate prospective strategy/skill edits; evidence cards, held-out tests and atomic ledger commit make accepted changes reachable by future worker snapshots (S4=A, bounded future operating-capability adaptation). Although parallel workers may contend on candidate diff keys and DefaultConflict/DefaultFusion/ReflectiveFusion reconcile stale or contradictory versions, this regulates a single experimental artifact, not demonstrated interference between independently viable S1 divisions with S2 organizational feedback (S2=—). BudgetGovernor, worker health, scheduling and numerical acceptance do not own whole-current managerial commitments among separate operating divisions (S3=—), while a ThreeLayerVerifier and statistical audit are candidate quality gates rather than complementary independent operational-reality inspection correcting an established S3 (S3*=—). Frozen L0 oracle guards and developer-supplied reward/policy enforce constraints without first-party legitimate ultimate identity/purpose arbitration and returned governance (S5=—). This classification is source-wired for the shipped model-backed mode, not an empirical LLM benchmark, and excludes outside model-provider organization and downstream deployment governance.
638|harness-rs|Harness RS ships a Rust AgentLoop with model-selected tool calls, typed tool dispatch, feedback through tool results/sensors and acceptance checks into further model iterations (S1=A). An optional first-party SkillDistiller takes a successful multi-step Episode, asks a configured model to generalize a reusable prospective SKILL.md, and its explicit opt-in distill_and_write path validates and writes the skill into the standard loadable skills root for later configured task runs (S4=A, bounded opt-in future procedural capability, not a default automatically scheduled feature). Subagent tasks, generic job DAGs and scheduler do not evidence resolution of interference among independently viable S1 departments with returned mutual coordination (S2=—). Runtime Policy/L1–L3 approval, budgets and tool guards enforce caller-selected per-task constraints rather than own whole-current organizational management across S1 divisions (S3=—); AuditHook, receipts, sensor/acceptance events and telemetry do not provide independent operational-reality audit correcting a separate S3 manager (S3*=—). Security allow/deny, operator approval and immutable/validated skill controls do not constitute a first-party ultimate policy/identity legitimate-authority decision and returned binding governance (S5=—). Source-backed optional API assessment only; no Rust runtime/inference or performance testing was possible in the isolated no-cargo execution environment.
639|fennec-agent-runtime|Fennec Agent Runtime ships a Python/LangChain model-backed Session and recursive query loop. The model binds first-party tool definitions, selects tool calls, observes permission-filtered ToolMessages and background-child notifications, and determines the next task action (S1=A). SpawnAgentTool reuses the same query loop for child tasks; TaskOutput/TaskStop/SendMessage/inboxes and safe tool concurrency coordinate dependent tasks but lack evidence of interference regulation among independently viable operating S1 divisions with returned mutual behavior changes (S2=—). Session depth and permission modes, approvals and cancellation enforce app-supplied bounds rather than whole-current autonomous resource/priority management across viable S1 departments (S3=—). Hooks, tool errors and Pydantic structured output validate current-task operation and do not constitute independent operational-reality inspection correcting a separate S3 executive (S3*=—). Model-driven context compaction, conversation persistence and TODO tools maintain current operating context, not prospective environmental sensing/options/adopted future agent capabilities (S4=—). User-provided ask_callback and tool safety modes are not first-party ultimate policy/identity adjudication with durable constitutional return (S5=—). Source-pinned study/port assessment only; no live LangChain provider run and no verified SPDX license, and original external Claude Code features are not imported.
640|agentflow|AgentFlow exposes a first-party Python Graph/PipelineSpec and Orchestrator that prepare real model-backed Codex/Claude/Kimi/Pi coding-agent CLI tasks, transport task context/tools and record outputs/traces for subsequent graph nodes. In the shipped composed mode the configured model-backed agent chooses context-sensitive actions/tools and observes workspace feedback, while the external CLI owns its own inner model execution (S1=A). Optional PipelineSpec optimizer with n_run>1 creates actual prior-round node/trace reports, sends them to model-backed optimizer for prospective rewritten pipeline.py, reloads and structurally validates the edited graph, persists current_pipeline and launches the next graph round from the updated version (S4=A); no empirical success uplift, default automatic invocation or organization-wide durable rollout is proven. DAG dependencies, fanout/merge, failed-node retries and optional model-generated periodic cancel/rerun commands regulate tasks in one graph, not concrete interference between independently viable S1 business divisions with distinct mutual S2 adjustment (S2=—). Runner concurrency, run-store lifecycle and periodic node interventions are not whole-current enterprise managerial allocation of resources/obligations across viable S1s (S3=—), while trace reports/review nodes/quality checks are task correctness feedback and not independent reality inspection correcting an S3 management picture (S3*=—). Host graph design, tool/sandbox/network and credential policy settings bound execution but are not an agent-owned legitimate ultimate-purpose/identity adjudication with returned binding governance (S5=—). Source-backed first-party integration only; live external coding-agent CLI provider task execution and upstream Python suite were not attested in the isolated environment.
641|recuris|Recuris wires a frozen downstream model into a first-party TurnRuntime via the tau2 RecurisAgent adapter: task input and grounded tool receipts update working memory and trigger skill delivery, model-backed drafts choose next task responses/tool calls, checkers can demand redraft, and later turns consume actual feedback (S1=A). An explicitly opt-in model-backed meta-agent campaign observes failing tasks, sanitized trajectories and memory-carrier activation fingerprints; proposes revisions to reusable experiential memory, working-memory schema, invocation rules or checkers; validates candidates against structural, leakage and activation safeguards; applies paired held-out significance and regression gates, and commits accepted memory versions for later downstream operating rounds (S4=A). Rejected candidates can be provisional working bases only under selected modes and are not confused with canonical acceptance or guaranteed improvement. Hierarchical parallel diagnosis workers and candidate consolidation optimize one future memory package rather than regulate concrete mutual interference between separately viable S1 operational divisions (S2=—). Task-level ledger update cadence, simulation budgets and model training-round controls are not whole-current managerial resource/priority authority across independent viable S1 divisions (S3=—). Grounded receipts, truth checkers, leakage/fingerprint controls and held-out benchmark selection protect current task and future candidate validity but do not provide a distinct complementary operational-reality audit that corrects an established S3 executive (S3*=—). Frozen research protocols, gate arithmetic and user-supplied task/benchmark/compute rules enforce externally chosen constraints instead of first-party legitimate ultimate-purpose/identity adjudication and binding returned policy (S5=—). Assessment is source-backed on published integration routes, not independently reproduced benchmark performance or live provider/model execution.
642|active-graph|Active Graph ships a first-party Python event-sourced runtime in which model-backed @llm_behavior actors react to scoped graph views and events. With an optional configured real LLMProvider, Runtime._invoke_llm calls the model, dispatches model-selected declared tools, returns actual tool-response messages to later model turns and applies resulting graph behavior patches, closing a context-sensitive operating loop (S1=A); the bundled Diligence offline quickstart was exercised successfully only with recorded model fixtures, not live inference. Typed relation behaviors, event subscriptions, optimistic patch rejection and serialized event queues regulate graph consistency; they do not establish a distinct source-backed agent owner that dampens concrete disturbances among independently viable S1 operating divisions and returns mutual adjustments (S2=—). Frames, execution budgets, scheduler/authority rules and configured policies enforce run constraints rather than exercising discretionary present managerial control over a portfolio of viable S1 units (S3=—). An append-only causal log, trace, strict replay, fork/diff and error events are evidence infrastructure, not an independent complementary operational-reality inspector correcting a separate S3 management picture and changing current decisions (S3*=—). Replay caches and caller-directed graph forks compare histories/structural hypotheses but lack a first-party model-owned future environmental intelligence, prospective strategy selection and adopted durable changes for later operating versions (S4=—). Tool capability ceilings, approval events and policy guards execute externally configured permissions but do not decide ultimate organizational identity/purpose under legitimate S5 authority with binding returned policy (S5=—). All mappings are source-bounded at the declared generic graph application recursion, not an empirical external model benchmark or a claim about third-party packs.
643|tinyagents|TinyAgents provides a first-party Rust AgentHarness model/tool run loop; registered external model decisions yield contextual tool names/arguments, first-party typed admission and dispatch executes effects, and tool results/errors return into the same model context before the next act or answer (S1=A). Separate crates for durable child tasks, team/workflow orchestration, typed graph fanout, resumable sessions and completion redelivery support delegated task operations, but do not exhibit a concrete regulator of interference between independently viable organizational S1 departments with reciprocal revised commitments (S2=—). Host-defined model/tool authorization, cost/turn limits, detached-task lifecycle and session control enforce task-level constraints and do not grant a first-party whole-current cross-S1 managerial resource/priority decision and return (S3=—). Transcripts, receipts, graph checkpoint/replay, tool validation and telemetry can support correctness but do not form an independently mandated complementary operational-reality audit that corrects a separate S3 executive (S3*=—). Model switches, persistent sessions, host tool registration and graph replay do not supply a shipped model-authored prospective external strategy/change option with accepted durable future operating capability return (S4=—). AgentDefinition explicitly reserves identity, prompts, authorization and higher organizational policy to hosts, while harness permission bounds cannot decide ultimate legitimate organizational purpose and return constitutional rules (S5=—). Source-backed on supported Rust model/tool routes; Rust provider inference and full upstream cargo tests were not independently executed in this environment.
