659|multi-agent-orchestrator-rs|The shipped Rust orchestrate and orchestrate-batch binaries integrate two distinct preexisting Claude/OpenCode/Codex sessions through first-party prompts, provider subprocess adapters, file transport, workspace checks and an FSM. A model-backed executor decides actual task investigation, plan or implementation; a separate model-backed reviewer inspects repository reality and returns accept/revise/blocked YAML, and a deterministic gate retries the executor or closes the same software task (S1=A). Provider sessions must be provisioned and authenticated externally; Rust code does not itself infer. The executor and reviewer are complementary roles within one coding work cell, not independently viable continuing S1 operations with reciprocal organizational disturbance coordination (S2=—). FSM limits, reviewer quality acceptance and operator flags govern the current task, not an ongoing managerial resource/accountability relationship across viable units (S3=—). The strong independent reviewer audits task deliverables and executor claims, not a distinct S3 manager's ordinary organizational reporting with a corrective S3 return (S3*=—). User-scoped investigate/plan workflow artifacts and provider options do not accept future organizational capability strategy (S4=—). Preconfigured permission, network restrictions and hard-stop outcomes are enforcement rather than legitimate ultimate identity-policy adjudication and return (S5=—).
