699|cluxmate|CluxMate's model-driven Python agent decides coding/tool actions and revises them from returned observations (S1=A). The parent model partitions delegated children and declares write-path scopes; a shipped scheduler serializes overlapping writers and returns coordination outcomes for replanning (S2=A). The parent model manages current child work while the attended operator can inspect and steer local whole-session state (S3=A(P)). A built-in separately prompted reviewer inspects requirements against actual workspace code/tests rather than implementer summaries and returns evidence-based findings for correction and scoped re-review; triggering and provider remain correlated with parent control (S3*=A). The model proactively records reusable external project/environment lessons in persistent AGENTS.md, injected on later turns as bounded prospective capability adaptation (S4=A). Trusted-workspace identity, permissions and ultimate risk policy remain owned by the human operator (S5=P).
700|aadixc0de-rune|AadiXC0DE's Rune has a model-selected native coding/tool loop and feedback-driven plan revision (S1=A, S3=A within one local session). Its exported child registry enforces per-parent uniqueness, capacity and bounded feedback, but the standard CLI registers `Subagent::unsupported` and only a host-composed constructor closes multi-child operation (S2=C, not A). The one-shot diff review and optional model permission reviewer do not independently audit S3 management claims (S3*=—). Static AGENTS.md/skills/session context and maintainer roadmaps do not form a prospective adaptation loop (S4=—). User-level permission, provider and profile policy overrides are protected against untrusted project config and govern subsequent native actions (S5=P).
701|termagent|TermAgent's first-party model/tool loop selects coding actions and revises them from execution and verification feedback (S1=A). Independent background coding agents operate within explicit non-overlapping write scopes; the parent model chooses parallel work partitions while the scheduler enforces conflict-specific admission and returns errors for replanning (S2=A). The parent model receives task IDs and individual status/output and can start or cancel current child commitments autonomously in auto-approval mode, whereas an interactive operator authorizes those changes in ask mode (S3=A(P)); a one-call fleet dashboard is absent. Mandatory verify_project remains ordinary production QA without a separately owned complementary auditor (S3*=—). Stored context and provider/skill/plugin configuration do not close future-environment capability adaptation (S4=—), and approval/profile settings do not close ultimate-policy or identity governance (S5=—).
702|cast|Cast's model-directed coding loop and independent subagents close S1=A. Serialized bash confirmations dampen actual shared-terminal contention among child S1s without agent-owned arbitration (S2=C). The attended local web operator sees and cancels current child commitments (S3=P), while a separate built-in reviewer child inspects code evidence and returns findings for correction (S3*=A). Optional model-backed distillation makes repeated project workflows available as future skills/personas (S4=A); permissions and configuration do not constitute ultimate-policy closure (S5=—).
703|glm-code|GLM Code's model/tool loop chooses coding actions and revises them from execution feedback (S1=A). Although the README advertises a Task subagent, the shipped tool registry omits it and the dispatcher rejects it before child execution; Bash jobs and ordered tool calls do not establish inter-S1 coordination (S2=—). Plan approval, todos, session state and job termination do not establish whole-current S3 or complementary S3* audit (both —). One-off AGENT.md project notes, web fetch and context compaction do not close prospective capability adaptation (S4=—), while project trust and permission modes enforce operator-supplied rules without an identity-policy return circuit (S5=—).
704|ranapriyansh-agent-loop|In agent-loop's documented opt-in --use-llm mode, one model-guided test–patch–retest loop selects code corrections from fresh failure feedback (S1=A); its default arithmetic-pattern fixer is deterministic and alone would not meet that state. One sequential Agent and test subprocesses do not establish distinct interacting S1s or an S2 coordination circuit (S2=—). Fixed iteration/test limits and user-selected task restrictions do not amount to whole-current S3 control (S3=—). Tests and append-only audit events feed ordinary production verification, not a separate complementary auditor (S3*=—). Current fixes do not revise future capabilities from external prospects (S4=—), and command/path constraints are not legitimate ultimate-policy governance (S5=—).
705|scout|Scout's first-party model/tool conversation chooses coding actions and revises them from execution feedback (S1=A). Concurrent tools are controlled by that same model, while /fork duplicates a session history rather than launching a child agent; no distinct-S1 interference-damping relation is shipped (S2=—). CLI settings, turn limits and event usage/status lack a whole-current S3 decision scope (S3=—). A repository-local Topo plugin can collect human HTML plan comments but does not provide an independent audit of management claims with corrective S3 return (S3*=—). Skills/plugin loading and stored history do not own prospective capability adaptation (S4=—), and project instructions/provider choices do not close legitimate ultimate-policy governance (S5=—).
706|ada|Ada's model-controlled coding and delegated agent loops choose tools and revise work from observations (S1=A). Distinct isolated child agents can collide on changed file paths; the parent-worktree reservation and copy-back filter dampen that concrete interference, but enforce a deterministic rule rather than agent-owned negotiation (S2=C). The model can inspect and steer a current same-process agent population, while an attended operator can inspect and cancel spawned background jobs in an alternative first-party mode (S3=A(P), with bounded visibility). Same-agent edit verification, test scripts and enterprise audit logs do not supply independent complementary audit with S3 return (S3*=—). In optional toolsmith mode, models read newly connected external MCP tool schemas, develop prospective capability-specific skills and persist them for future use (S4=A). Enterprise policy, permissions and operator approval do not establish ultimate identity/policy deliberation and returned S5 closure (S5=—).
707|woopcode|Woopcode's single model/tool coding loop chooses tools and revises actions using returned execution observations (S1=A). Batched tool calls, session-history forks and sandbox processes are not distinct autonomous S1 units with a shipped interference-damping S2 circuit (S2=—). Its one-turn cancellation, iteration/wall budgets and attended approvals do not provide a whole-current multi-agent management view and resource/commitment control (S3=—). Mandatory diff review and unattended finish gates send verification/requirement reminders back to the SAME model, rather than an independent auditor with S3 corrective return (S3*=—). Web search, plan mode and retained task/execution context serve current work but do not create an outside-and-then capability adaptation loop (S4=—). Tool approval policies, safe sandboxing and user mode choices enforce local permissions without closed ultimate identity-policy governance (S5=—).
708|nanocoder|Nanocoder's interactive, delegated and optionally event-triggered model/tool agent runs choose repository actions and revise them from tool feedback (S1=A). The optional first-party daemon deterministically drops overlapping events for one subscription and debounces file-change bursts, damping concrete cross-run collision/overload only within that subscription (S2=C); ordinary parallel children are not so protected. Delegation, UI progress and a shared abort lack a whole-current selective resource or commitment management decision circuit (S3=—). Custom reviewers, hooks, ordinary tests and checkpoints do not close independent complementary audit through S3 (S3*=—). Memory, plans, extensible skills and reactive events do not establish a shipped external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop (S4=—). User modes, tool approvals and maintainers' release decisions do not supply closed ultimate-policy/identity governance for an operating Nanocoder session (S5=—).
709|yottacode|yottacode's model-backed coding and child-agent loops decide repository tool actions using execution feedback (S1=A). Opt-in experimental dispatch deterministically rejects overlapping write-file ownership across concurrently running subagents/batches and confines each write worker to a separate worktree/file set (S2=C), unlike ordinary Agent fan-out. The attended TUI operator sees the entire current local-session subagent roster and can selectively stop one running child or a named batch (S3=P); decomposition and branch integration alone do not make S3 autonomous. An on-demand code-review fan-out returns critique to the originating producing workflow rather than a separately governed complementary audit and current-control closure (S3*=—). Research/planning/memory do not drive an externally prospective capability redesign dialogue (S4=—), and approval modes and safety defaults are tactical tool policies rather than ultimate organizational identity governance (S5=—).
710|keen-code|Keen Code's main and optionally delegated model-backed agents make tool choices and react to repository observations and child results (S1=A). Its `delegate_task` parallelizes up to ten separately deciding workers and returns an ordered result barrier, but explicitly does not enforce cross-agent file ownership, worktree isolation, scheduling or shared budget control (S2=—). Parent task decomposition, per-profile timeouts and all-child cancellation lack a current whole-cohort portfolio decision and selective intervention return (S3=—). Review profiles exist only as user-authored examples, and ordinary tests/approvals do not create independent complementary S3* audit with S3 closure (S3*=—). Web/MCP research, memory, skills and plans serve current tasks without a prospective capability adaptation dialogue (S4=—). Permission modes and filesystem guards gate individual tools without legitimate ultimate organizational identity decisions (S5=—).
711|stemcode|StemCode's first-party coding agent and delegated child sessions choose repository tool operations from model/tool feedback (S1=A). Its primary model owns the task-specific split into distinct child agents and selects a coordination strategy/write scopes, while shipped orchestration serializes editing-capable children and permits parallel read-only ones to attenuate shared-workspace mutation interference (S2=A, bounded to a single awaited handoff). Plans, sessions, budgets and result aggregation do not yield persistent whole-current priority/resource intervention over an independently running cohort (S3=—). A shipped optional PR-review automation path constructs a separate read-only agent review of base/head diffs and publishes its findings, but autonomous corrective feedback to the coding controller is not wired (S3*=C constructor only). Web/codebase intelligence and lesson memory do not close future-oriented capability adaptation (S4=—); runtime profiles, approval rules and maintainers' governance do not constitute legitimate ultimate policy/identity closure for a running coding session (S5=—).
712|orca-code|Orca Code's shipped Go CLI/REPL uses one model-driven coding/tool loop to select repository operations and revise choices from fresh tool and verification feedback (S1=A). Sequential tool dispatch and shell subprocesses are not independently deciding operational S1 actors, so no cross-agent coordination is established (S2=—). Cost/turn caps and permission modes do not own current whole-system priorities (S3=—); trusted post-edit verification returns to the same model, not to an independent complementary auditor (S3*=—). Product-research documents and a planned future daemon/swarm architecture do not implement prospective adaptive intelligence (S4=—). Human action approvals and static trust restrictions do not close legitimate system identity governance (S5=—).
