714|alisio|Alisio's first-party CLI runs a model-directed coding loop and default-enabled independently deciding child agents (S1=A). The primary model selects child tasks, profiles and later coordination messages, while a shipped collision-specific guard detects competing write-capable children and serializes or isolates their repository mutations (S2=A, bounded to the parent session); an interactive operator owns the worktree-versus-serial safety selection. Child status, token caps and queues do not establish a separate whole-current S3 commitment-control decision (S3=—). An example user-supplied reviewer is not a wired complementary independent auditor (S3*=—). Long-term SQLite memory and agent configuration do not close externally prospective capability adaptation (S4=—). Permission prompts and worktree policy are tactical constraints, not identity/ultimate-policy closure (S5=—).
715|karmx|karmX's Goose-derived coding runtime and independently delegated model/tool sessions make and revise coding decisions from execution feedback (S1=A). The sidekick wrapper invokes ordinary delegation, and advisory file-partitioning warnings without a concrete conflict-specific coordinated return do not prove S2 (S2=—). In a shipped opt-in orchestrator mode, a top-level model inspects a whole-current roster of peer sessions and chooses start/message/interrupt interventions that change active commitments (S3=A). Lead diff reading, browser preview and unit tests do not establish a distinct independent complementary auditor with corrective S3 feedback (S3*=—). Native context retrieval, prompt enhancement, browser/CDP work and compaction support present coding rather than future capability adaptation (S4=—); permissions and user configuration are not ultimate identity-policy closure (S5=—).
716|likecc|LikeCC's first-party model/tool coding loop and independent child workers select edits, tests and follow-up work from feedback (S1=A). Worktree isolation, task state and messages lack a demonstrated interference-specific inter-worker corrective return (S2=—). Dual opt-in coordinator mode supplies a whole-current control constructor, but its advertised follow-up and stop tools are not registered to the lead (S3=C). A separately enabled fresh-context Verification agent can inspect changed artifacts and send an independent verdict into another model-directed correction (S3*=A in the supported opt-in path, not a mandatory gate). Session/memory facilities do not close future-environment capability adaptation (S4=—), and permissions do not close ultimate identity policy (S5=—).
717|k-cli|K-CLI's model-backed coding and debugger loops choose implementation and repairs from tool/verification feedback (S1=A). Parallel subagent DAGs, patch aggregation and separately invoked Git conflict resolution do not establish interference-specific correction among distinct K-CLI workers (S2=—); sequential personas and deterministic worker/budget scheduling do not own whole-current commitments (S3=—). An optional separate PR reviewer reads real diff evidence and can publish findings later consumed by a separately invoked PR-fix model, exposing a composable independent audit constructor rather than mandatory closure (S3*=C). Persistent memory, model routing and retrieval support current coding rather than prospective capability adaptation (S4=—); action policy and operator approvals do not close ultimate identity governance (S5=—).
718|cortexterm|CortexTerm's installed CLI/TTY model loop chooses repository tools and subsequent coding actions from their returned results (S1=A). Its sub-agent lifecycle module is not connected to first-party execution, and same-session task/background-status facilities do not regulate interference among distinct S1 units (S2=—) or supply whole-current control (S3=—). Built-in code-review and governance-audit tools return deterministic checks to that same coding model, not independent complementary audit and corrective return (S3*=—). Pi-style compaction, retained memory and optional MCP/skills extend present task context but do not close future capability adaptation (S4=—); user action permissions and static rules do not decide ultimate identity policy (S5=—).
