720|sangxia-ai|Sangxia.ai's ACP/TUI coding agent selects tools and revises work from returned results (S1=A). Session-local serialization, plan display and optional MCP connections do not coordinate distinct S1 agents (S2=—), and timeouts, cancel/approval controls and user hooks do not close separate current management (S3=—) or independent complementary audit (S3*=—). Distinctively, a model-owned first-project initialization turn inspects the repository, synthesizes durable AGENTS.md and .sangxia/memory.md guidance, and reloads it for subsequent work (S4=A); authentication and tactical permissions do not govern ultimate identity (S5=—).
721|yagent|Yagent's local Go model/tool loop and bounded read-only subagents choose task operations from tool feedback (S1=A), while child fan-out, shared scratch and provider slots do not furnish an inter-S1 conflict-control return (S2=—). Goal/verification gates and UI controls are task-level, not separate whole-current management (S3=—). The optional fresh-context `auditor` child is a complementary review constructor without mandatory finding-to-repair closure (S3*=C). Complex-turn skill distillation produces lasting model-authored procedures directly in default mode or only after optional operator staging/review (S4=A(P)); auth and permissions are not ultimate identity policy (S5=—).
722|sanook-cli|Sanook CLI's parent and child model/tool loops select coding operations from tool feedback (S1=A). The primary model can select `task_parallel(isolate=true)` to protect concurrently writing children in independent Git worktrees, followed by sequential patch checks and collision reports for further decisions (S2=A, bounded to this optional mode). The parent sees the entire in-session background task roster and can selectively cancel a running child (S3=A); a separate complementary audit role is not shipped (S3*=—). After a successful multi-step task the model can choose to save a reusable skill for anticipated future tasks, automatically discoverable in later turns (S4=A, bounded to skill-based adaptation); tactical approvals and persona configuration do not close ultimate identity policy (S5=—).
723|tesseractcli-agent|TesseractCLI-Agent runs one model-owned ReAct loop over workspace tool schemas and returns approved or denied tool results to subsequent model decisions (S1=A). Alternative inference models are fallback candidates for that one operating agent, not separate coordinating cells (S2=—); iteration limits and human tool approvals do not create an independent whole-current management owner (S3=—). Neither unit tests nor tracing constitute complementary independent operational audit (S3*=—). SQLite transcript memory, lazy tool schema loading and provider pack settings service current sessions without prospective adopted capability change (S4=—), while static sandbox constraints and the prompt's identity text do not decide ultimate organizational policy (S5=—).
