731|xeyo|XEYO's Python coding agent and its independently acting, scoped subagents perform model-selected workspace tasks (S1=A). A shared write store rejects stale overlapping edits, and optional shipped coord worktree workers use claim/scope leases to attenuate conflicting S1 actions with actionable outcomes (S2=A). A lead model delegates and responds to child results; the first-party server permits a human to inspect, cancel and send follow-ups to live children (S3=A(P)). Runtime completion diagnostics are nonblocking, while a coord Reviewer only enforces externally supplied findings; no independent in-product audit return is closed (S3*=—). Web tools, memory, planning-module names and developer benchmarks do not establish prospective organizational adaptation (S4=—), and permission/role controls do not adjudicate XEYO's ultimate identity (S5=—).
732|aura-code|Aura Code's model-driven CLI and real specialist and child model/tool loops perform workspace tasks from current evidence (S1=A). Its optional model-built specialist dependency DAG and actionable reviewer-blocking feedback coordinate distinct workers and bounded repair (S2=A), although parallel board swarm writes have no shared file locks. A lead model delegates, tracks current specialist outcomes and adapts the task plan's execution; the first-party board separately gives an operator live task control (S3=A(P)). An independent readonly reviewer model returns a structured blocking verdict and can trigger coder rework and review reruns, when included in the specialist plan (S3*=A, optional mode; verdict parsing can fail open). Opt-in Archimedes uses retained verified outcomes to select, verify, escalate and prospectively probe task-pattern model capability through deterministic policy (S4=C); standalone harness-patch commands do not generalize that function. Permission, model and board settings remain task-scoped and do not close ultimate identity policy (S5=—).
