739|ucode|ucode's main and optional parallel worker model/tool loops select and revise repository actions from real workspace feedback (S1=A). For up to three concurrently coding worker S1 actors, one deterministic Promise-chain lock serializes file writes and shell commands and returns their actual outcomes, attenuating common project contention without an autonomous coordination owner (S2=C). Task delegation, plans and finished worker summaries do not form a distinct whole-current organizational resource manager (S3=—). The optional operator-triggered /look path supplies browser screenshots and a separate vision-model specialist critique to the main Agent's later control (S3*=A); automatic app checks explicitly omit that model review. Provider fallbacks and maintainer trace optimizations are not first-party future-capability governance (S4=—), and scope/deploy confirmations do not decide ultimate system identity/policy (S5=—).
740|hand|Hand's shipped Go model/tool loop performs user-directed workspace coding with adaptive source/tool feedback (S1=A). Its single active application operation, session forks and background process handling do not instantiate multiple autonomous S1 work cells with mutual-interference coordination (S2=—), nor a separate whole-current commitment manager (S3=—). Mandatory verification profiles recapture workspace evidence and check command outcomes, but they are ordinary configured verification rather than an independent complementary auditor (S3*=—). Model fallback, web access, user-installed packages and hooks do not close prospective capability-adaptation governance (S4=—); scoped permissions, trust gates and user configuration do not adjudicate ultimate policy or identity (S5=—).
741|cox|cox's Rust main and parallel child model/tool Sessions make coding and investigation decisions from actual workspace outcomes (S1=A). The main model can select per-child isolated Git worktrees to attenuate competing writable operations, and the child's branch/result feeds later work; deterministic task messaging is supporting coordination, not a separate default sibling agent team (S2=A). A parent model can see concurrent child job starts, completions and returned messages, allocate bounded budgets and redirect or resume active commitments (S3=A, scoped to the current child team). Generic explore subagents and developer evaluations are not independent operational audit (S3*=—). Model tier routing and extracted memory are not prospective capability adoption (S4=—), and permissions/configuration do not settle ultimate system identity policy (S5=—).
742|ambient-cli|Ambient CLI's main and separate bounded scout/oracle/builder model/tool loops select workspace actions from real tool feedback (S1=A). Its deterministic serialized approval queue resolves contention among parallel child requests (S2=C), but role dispatch and wave completion do not create a separate standing whole-current management owner (S3=—). An optional read-only oracle child independently inspects source and reports a specialist critique (S3*=A). Live model-fleet readiness and accumulated request reliability can alter future model selection under fixed heuristics (S4=C, narrow capability-optioning). The interactive human owner can approve or rewrite the durable session north-star through first-party goal proposals and persisted goal updates (S5=P, session-level only); no live LLM efficacy was tested.
743|rohanasudani-termagent|TermAgent's standard CLI runs one model-directed coding/tool loop with actual workspace and verifier observations, preview-hash write gates, and bounded recovery (S1=A). Interactive requests instantiate sequential rather than coordinated parallel agents, and external benchmark copies are not a runtime worker team (S2=—). Task-plan, transition and cost guards are fixed controls around that S1 rather than standing whole-current management (S3=—). Strict completion review is authored by the same model; independent pristine grading occurs only in separate experiment workflows, not an embedded runtime auditor (S3*=—). Fixed provider selection/retries and developer campaigns do not form prospective capability governance (S4=—). User task flags and command approvals are operational limits, not a durable ultimate-identity adjudication channel (S5=—).
744|swarms-code|A Rust terminal coding agent wraps the swarms-rs model/tool API in its own repeated history-aware coding loop with registered file/search/Bash tools and optional autonomous approval mode (S1=A). It instantiates one coding S1, not an inter-S1 coordinator or whole-current manager (S2=—, S3=—). File checks and command output stay in ordinary production feedback rather than independent audit (S3*=—). Provider/catalog selection does not close prospective capability adaptation (S4=—), and prompts/tool approvals do not supply identity-level policy adjudication (S5=—).
745|davecode|DaveCode ships a first-party built-in model/tool coding loop and goal-artifact work cells with real workspace feedback (S1=A). A shared in-process workspace lock attenuates conflicting Git writes by separately startable runner and goal operations, but does not autonomously reschedule them (S2=C, narrowly scoped). The goal reviewer sees the whole current task portfolio and revises retries/new work, with operator task-graph controls as a separate parent mode (S3=A(P)). Optional dedicated Jev inspection of actual staged diffs and validation results may reject code into the repair cycle (S3*=A; default judge is none). Routing/repair do not create prospective capability governance (S4=—), while configuration and task controls do not decide ultimate organizational policy (S5=—).
747|durable-harness|durable-harness's published Pi mode constructs a model-backed agent using supported Pi peer-loop software and registers its first-party coding workspace tools; tool results return to the model across World-backed durable turns (S1=A, only this composed Pi mode). World queues, session leases and crash replay enforce single-session integrity, not demonstrated inter-S1 coordination (S2=—) or a whole-current multi-agent manager (S3=—). Tracing and checkpoints are not independent review (S3*=—). Recovery/configurable agent factories do not prospectively redesign capabilities (S4=—), and public-route auth/sandbox budgets are configured enforcement rather than identity-policy adjudication (S5=—). External Claude/Codex/OpenCode agent loops are excluded from this first-party attribution.
749|harness-cli|Harness² CLI ships an executable model-backed harness editing/probe/select loop for user tasks (S1=A). Concurrency and domain scheduling are experiment mechanics, not S2 or S3 governance (S2=—, S3=—); normal selection and optional evaluation do not provide a separately independent S3* audit (S3*=—). Model-backed task adoption, reflection and domain experience reuse inform later harness adaptation; its separately supported human-blind-selection mode returns the parent's candidate choice into the same future library (S4=A(P)). CLI configuration and reviewed apply are not ultimate identity/policy settlement (S5=—). This fork reuses the already-indexed Google Research Harness² core but adds a distinct deployable CLI and parent selection mode; no new benchmark gains are claimed.
750|autoresearchlab|AutoResearchLab's first-party model-backed scientific role lanes perform scoped research work (S1=A), under LabDirector's agent-owned whole-cycle decisions to refine, pivot, conclude and schedule follow-ups (S3=A). Independently dispatched Gatekeepers can reject novelty, quality or reproducibility claims back into the current research cycle (S3*=A). Field-landscape evidence and gated candidate generation prospectively revise the lab's research agenda, with a supported researcher-selection mode (S4=A(P)); this is not demonstrated autonomous improvement of harness code. A message bus and hierarchical handoffs do not establish distinct inter-S1 interference attenuation (S2=?), and researcher instructions or venue selection do not constitute independent ultimate identity/policy closure (S5=—). Unlike neighboring harness-optimization systems, its prospective adaptation target is the scientific question pursued by the lab; no live research-cycle outcome was measured.
751|knowwork|KnowWork deploys model-directed user task/tool S1 by composing an official DeepSeek Harness agent with its own enterprise host, model gateway, tool bridge, permission context and sandbox execution (S1=A for the installed composition, not an independently authored DSH loop). Distinct concurrent user sessions receive collision-checked `(workspace, session)` Docker identities and isolated file/action worlds, yielding narrow first-party constructor S2=C against cross-session interference; the no-sandbox mode is not credited. Author-fixed workflow scheduling, routine completion gates and replay/audit logs do not supply whole-current managerial S3 or independently complementary S3* audit (S3=—, S3*=—). Skill/model administration and upgrade preflight are configuration/maintenance rather than outside-and-then intelligence (S4=—), while RBAC grants and per-tool human approvals enforce operating permissions rather than ultimate identity policy (S5=—).
753|agenticseek|AgenticSeek ships specialist code/file/browser model/tool feedback loops (S1=A) and a supported HIGH-complexity Planner route whose own model decomposes the whole current task, dispatches specialist roles, reads their results and revises outstanding commitments after success/failure (S3=A, bounded to the multi-specialist user task rather than a persistent fleet). Router selection, sequential delegation and `need` dependency transfer do not close a distinct sibling interference-attenuation function (S2=—). Interpreter failures and worker-status returns are ordinary operations/S3 management feedback, not separately independent audit (S3*=—). Fixed startup classifier examples, prompt/provider configuration and local replanning do not adopt prospective external capability changes (S4=—); operator-controlled prompts/safety are not ultimate identity-policy settlement (S5=—).
754|phone-harness|Phone Harness provides an installable Hermes plugin that composes an external model-directed actor with first-party phone scripts, actual device observations and returned tool results (S1=A for the supported composition, not a Phone Harness-authored LLM loop); a plugin-process mutex and busy feedback specifically attenuate simultaneous Hermes subagent input collisions at one phone (S2=C), unlike general tool routing, without giving the harness autonomous whole-current management (S3=—), independent complementary audit (S3*=—), prospective environmental adaptation (S4=—) or runtime ultimate-policy closure (S5=—).
755|routa|Routa's installed provider-backed CRAFTER/GATE agents act on repository tasks and tool feedback (S1=A). Shipped specialist conflict preflight and cross-agent responses allow model-directed adjustment of concurrent implementors (S2=A, subject to prompt compliance; queue limits alone are deterministic), while a ROUTA coordinator sees active agents and task reports and selects implementation, verification and repair waves (S3=A). A separately spawned evidence-reading GATE verifier independently judges implementor claims and returns APPROVED/NOT_APPROVED/BLOCKED into work routing (S3*=A; role/session separation is not provider independence). Retrospective playbooks and provider setup do not establish prospective outside-and-then capability adaptation (S4=—); initial human plan approval and routine sandbox/agent permissions do not settle ultimate organizational policy (S5=—). This is an assessment of the packaged composed agent mode, with no live model performance claim.
756|edict|Edict installs a first-party suite of specialized roles into a pre-existing OpenClaw runtime: model-backed ministries choose task actions and return outcomes (S1=A), while Shangshu assigns current edict work across ministries and synthesizes reports (S3=A). A separate Menxia role assesses Zhongshu's plan, can veto and return it for replanning (S3*=A, explicitly **limited** by the shipped mandatory third-round approval rule). Ministry routing, allowlists and an optional unproven-in-default-mode Redis dispatch semaphore do not establish actual inter-S1 conflict attenuation (S2=—). A morning global-news digest and manual model/skill changes do not form a future-capability adaptation loop (S4=—); imperial names, initial user commands and ordinary task permissions do not establish identity/ultimate-policy governance (S5=—). Docker's no-OpenClaw simulated dashboard is excluded from credited autonomous operation.
757|ownpilot|OwnPilot's packaged Claw agent chooses actions and subsequent tool calls in a configurable mission/cycle runtime (S1=A). First-party parent/child Claws mandate separate child Docker isolation against workspace corruption, and the crew queue atomically blocks rival agents claiming one task (S2=C for scoped inter-S1 interference, not autonomous coordination discretion). A model-backed parent Claw can spawn subclaws, inspect all its children and stop selected work on live feedback (S3=A for that parent mission, not full user-account fleet management). Optional crew request_review is merely peer messaging and run reliability stats are normal diagnostics, not mandatory independent complementary audit (S3*=—). Learned task skills are retrospective, not externally prospective capability strategy (S4=—), and owner-set missions, permissions and tool approvals do not close ultimate identity policy (S5=—). No model-run effectiveness was tested.
759|pi-multi-agent|Pi-Multi-Agent wires model-backed tool and response loops into operational work cells (S1=A). Its opt-in debate mode feeds competing agent claims into later rounds and an LLM consensus synthesis (narrow S2=C constructor), while the deep-mode goal team reviews current task outputs and deterministically upgrades weak-task priority and prompts for another iteration (narrow S3=C constructor, not agent-owned global management). Production quality critics do not provide an independent complementary audit (S3*=—), current-goal replanning and model routing do not establish external-prospective adaptation (S4=—), and config/prompt/budget enforcement does not return a binding identity-policy resolution (S5=—). The vector describes shipped selectable modes; no live LLM outcome is claimed.
761|kode-sdk|KODE SDK's exported model-backed Agent repeatedly chooses tools based on observations and returns results through its persistent TypeScript runtime (S1=A). AgentPool, Room and task_run support multi-agent messaging, lifecycle and delegation but do not attenuate evidenced sibling interference (S2=—) or revise commitments/resources from a whole-current operating view (S3=—). Logs, permission checks and example reviewers do not establish independent complementary audit (S3*=—); runtime skill scanning and operator capability configuration do not close an external-prospective adaptation loop (S4=—); per-tool API approval and template constraints do not provide identity-level governance (S5=—). The reviewed boundary is the shipped SDK, not a downstream application; no live model experiment was run.
762|langroid|Langroid ships an autonomous model-to-tool/task-feedback loop in ChatAgent and Task (S1=A). Its default task loop can detect repeated Alice/Bob subtask NO_ANSWER oscillation and stop the remaining inter-agent turns (narrow deterministic S2=C constructor); ordinary round-robin routing and delegation alone are not S2. Static task limits and one-query LanceRAG planner/critic iterations do not create whole-current governance of a continuing operational population (S3=—). The first-party critic sees a reported plan and answer, without complementary independent source access or audit-to-control return (S3*=—). Present-goal query adjustment, user-selected providers/prompts and ordinary task approval/quit do not establish prospective strategic capability adaptation (S4=—) or ultimate identity-policy settlement (S5=—). No live LLM tests were run.
763|bub|Bub's first-party model-backed Agent/BubAgent selects tools and continues from returned observations and tape context (S1=A). Its built-in subagent invokes another session on the same runtime and awaits its output; channel admission can steer, queue, drop or pass incoming messages but default admission permits concurrency, without evidenced conflict-specific attenuation among distinct agents (S2=—). Session queues, subtask calls and cancellation do not establish a whole-current manager revising population commitments (S3=—); event traces and tool hooks are not independent evidence-reading audit-to-control (S3*=—). Operator-configured skills, tools, model settings and present-task web fetch are not a prospective adaptation loop (S4=—), nor do allowlists, tool-call gates and interactive CLI input constitute ultimate policy authority (S5=—). Assessment covers shipped first-party code; no live LLM-agent tests were run.
764|boop|Boop wires a model-backed iMessage dispatcher to restricted tool-bearing execution work cells and feeds their result into the user conversation (S1=A); provider SDK cognition itself is external. Spawn/cron, shared memory, webhook dedup and staged drafts do not regulate a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict (S2=—). Job dashboards, retry and stale-heartbeat enforcement do not close whole-current discretionary control (S3=—); adversarial memory-consolidation QA checks maintenance proposals rather than independently auditing operational claims into S3 (S3*=—). Proactive email alerts and memory updates do not change prospective organizational capability (S4=—), and user send approval/tool restrictions do not adjudicate ultimate identity policy (S5=—). Source-only assessment; no live model run.
765|argue|argue's standard CLI wires multiple configured model participants through its first-party round/claim/vote feedback loop to produce an autonomous deliberation result (S1=A); external model/provider inference is not claimed as first-party internals. Conflicting or duplicate participant claims are merged/revised into a shared active catalog and returned with peer objections to later discussion rounds, supplying a narrow S2-specific reconciliation constructor (S2=C, not autonomous conflict-policy ownership). Fixed participant timeouts, score-based report representative selection and request-level rules do not revise whole-current operating commitments (S3=—). Ordinary peer critique and derived scores lack independent audit access or return into a separate current-control path (S3*=—); debate topics and configured model choices do not adapt the organization's future capability (S4=—), and votes on claims are not binding ultimate-identity deliberation (S5=—). Static pinned-source assessment, no live model tests.
766|codemachine-cli|CodeMachine CLI wires configured model-backed external coding engines through its runnable CLI/TUI, agent runner, workspace context and result/session return (S1=A), without crediting vendor CLI internals. Its parallel/sequential coordination DSL and monitoring hierarchy schedule work but supply no conflict-specific cross-S1 correction (S2=—). A pre-execution controller, step-back directives, deterministic loops and status/abort rules do not establish whole-current discretionary control over actual resource commitments (S3=—). Production logs/telemetry and optional custom review lack independent audit-to-control closure (S3*=—). Built-in workflow generation, provider fallback and template selection address current tasks and settings rather than external-prospective capability governance (S4=—). Interactive checkpoint approval/MCP gates do not constitute ultimate identity/policy resolution (S5=—). Static pinned-source assessment, no live model tests.
767|ai-coding-agents-orchestrator|AI Coding Agents Orchestrator's shipped Go TaskRunner coordinates first-party PM, Coder, QA, Security and UX model-backed operational agents with real source/test tools (S1=A). QA independently diagnoses failing implementation-versus-test expectations and directs test correction or Coder retries, closing a bounded inter-S1 conflict response (S2=A). The PM reads cross-role QA/Security/UX findings, arbitrates fixes and can create further task beads, feeding revised whole-current project commitments back to operational agents (S3=A within one project). The separately prompted Security reviewer reads real on-disk source files instead of relying on Coder self-report, returns security MustFix findings to PM arbitration and remediation (S3*=A, bounded to current-project code); author-QA reviewing its own tests is not credited as independent audit. Project memory, prompt routing and learned conventions do not show external-prospective capability adaptation (S4=—); human artifact approval gates do not exercise autonomous ultimate organizational identity/policy choice (S5=—). Source-only assessment; no live model tests.
768|kimchi|Kimchi's first-party multi-model default and AgentManager construct model-backed Builder/Fixer/Explorer workers with concrete source/test tools and returned work artifacts, while generic pi-mono session internals remain an external SDK dependency (S1=A). Queue caps, dependency phases, Ferment parallel-cohort checks and vertical reviewer-to-Fixer correction do not evidence an autonomous S2-specific settlement of an interference between distinct operational S1 units (S2=—). The current-job orchestrator model chooses plan/build/review phases, delegated commitments, repair after findings and a bounded stop (S3=A, one coding request). A separate Reviewer agent context can independently read actual source/spec and run tests/lint, issuing an evidence-backed report to orchestrator and another Fixer (S3*=A for that available path); trivial-task self-review is explicitly allowed and not counted, and prompt-only read-only instruction is not a secure filesystem boundary. Project research/memory, provider-side auto model picks and model-deprecation fallback do not return agent-governed prospective capability changes (S4=—), while user task/model/tool permissions are not ultimate identity-policy adjudication (S5=—). Pinned source only, no live LLM tests.
769|opencode-agent-orchestration-kit|OAK's default OpenCode lead directs model-backed research, specification and developer roles through task/tool feedback (S1=A), then selects current feature or opt-in autonomous phases, developer rework and bounded closure (S3=A for one coding mission). A separate read-only reviewer directly examines source/diff/tests and returns findings to the lead; the autonomous loop additionally requires a reviewer child-session attestation before terminal completion (S3*=A for the nontrivial review path, not proof of audit quality). Sequential barriers and journal leases do not settle a specific inter-S1 conflict (S2=—). Maintainer-only /evolve is restricted to OAK's own source checkout, not installed-project prospective capability governance (S4=—); owner permissions and PR merge restrictions are not ultimate policy arbitration (S5=—). Source-wiring assessment, no live model trial.
770|karimo|KARIMO packages model-backed Claude Code research, task preparation and source-changing workers (S1=A), with a model PM choosing current PRD wave assignments, review/rework and stoppage (S3=A). Its separate brief reviewer checks independently against actual code/config/PRD before implementation and returns findings to a different corrector (S3*=A for preparatory plan audit, not every code PR). Distinctively, the default preflight can detect inter-worker same-wave shared-file/dependency contradictions and a human-selected correction path changes subsequent briefs/tasks (S2=C, no autonomous live conflict arbitrator). External feature research and user-triggered retrospective learned rules do not constitute prospective organizational capability governance (S4=—); PRD approvals, gate prompts and GitHub merge rights do not settle ultimate identity policy (S5=—). First-party pinned prompt/role wiring only; no live model evaluation.
771|veritas-kanban|Veritas Kanban ships a local board with optional supervised execution of model-backed coding tasks in Veritas-owned worktrees, envelopes and provider adapters (S1=A in configured agent-ready mode, not board-only). Its PM prompt and SOP can form a discretionary sprint manager only after explicit human commissioning (S3=C, no automatic PM activation). A separately callable read-only Codex review thread reads the original worktree diff and persists findings, but operator/PM action is required for correction and the review verdict is not an automatic task closure gate (S3*=C). Admission queues, workflow order, messaging and native git conflict UI do not demonstrate a differentiated inter-S1 conflict owner (S2=—). Retrospective reflection/profile promotion is human-reviewed, not autonomous outside-and-then capability strategy (S4=—), and login/tool/approval policies are constraints, not ultimate identity-policy arbitration (S5=—). Pinned source wiring only; no live model test.
772|agetor|Agetor runs configured third-party model-backed coding CLIs on first-party managed task worktrees and pipeline steps, returning real file/tool outcomes (S1=A). Its pipeline handoffs may let a worker pick an allowed outgoing edge, but the first-party step prompt restricts the model role to its own step and the runner follows a user-authored graph; no whole-current autonomous S3 controller (S3=—). A user can compose a separate Review step with original worktree access and a feedback edge to the Implement step, producing a conditional complementary audit constructor (S3*=C, not an always-on reviewer). Per-card worktree isolation, shared-parent run locks, Git conflict UI and parallel sibling warnings do not decide inter-S1 semantic collisions (S2=—). Current model compatibility discovery is not prospective capability adaptation (S4=—); user CLI approvals and final PR rights are not ultimate policy governance (S5=—). Static pinned source, no live provider evaluation and no upstream Bun runtime in this environment.
773|harness-for-codex|Harness for Codex installs native Codex operational researcher/worker/QA roles with scoped source, test and tool decisions returned to the parent (S1=A); its parent also specifically resolves two-worker shared-file/interface collisions by assigning an exclusive owner and returning revised contracts to affected workers (S2=A, not generic chat or sequencing). The model-backed parent reads the current whole run's tasks, dependencies, evidence, capacity and failures and actively reallocates present work, integrates and directs rework (S3=A); a distinct read-only reviewer independently examines actual diffs/code and returns causal findings for parent-to-worker correction (S3*=A). Project-local role/skill creation and later on-demand drift maintenance are not externally prospective capability strategy (S4=—); human user purpose and inherited Codex permissions are not first-party ultimate-policy decisions (S5=—). Pinned first-party role/skill contract plus 97/97 deterministic native tests; no new live LLM run or audit-quality trial.
774|rustcode|RustCode owns a native Rust model/tool coding loop with optional isolated child workspaces (S1=A); child scopes, scheduling, verification and user approvals constrain work without supplying differentiated organizational metasystem closure.
776|learn-claude-code-rs|The integrated sfull tutorial runs its own Rust model/tool coding loop and synchronous fresh-context coding child (S1=A); named teammates and plan/shutdown messages are stored records, cron has no integrated due-task executor, and worktree CRUD does not supply organizational metasystem closure.
777|onecli|OneCLI runs model-backed hosted agents in isolated sandboxes with durable memory and tool feedback (S1=A). Its first-party peer-task contract specifically damps two-agent ping-pong and competing pair tasks through bounded budgets, a one-active pair fence and terminal return, but the collision response is deterministic/owner-controlled rather than autonomous mediation (S2=C). Workspace operators use a whole-current view to revise agent briefs, access grants and resources, enforced by runner and gateway (S3=P; enforcement alone is not agent discretion). Optional hosted evals directly test agent answers/app use and a separate no-tools model judge can report criterion failures; owner-mediated manual remediation, not auto-repair, limits the audit path to S3*=C. Scheduled work and regression monitoring are not outside-and-then strategic adaptation (S4=—); brief/policy edits are not an evidenced whole-workspace ultimate-identity adjudication (S5=—). OSS hosted distribution only; no live provider tests.
778|learnharness|LearnHarness runs model-backed adaptive prober, teacher and specialist research/demo/visual/PDF workers (S1=A). Its queues and locks serialize work but do not settle inter-S1 semantic conflicts (S2=—). In a specifically bounded **single live learning session**, the teacher owns a current learner-goal/plan view, revises pedagogical commitments and commissions specialists in response to learner checks and returned jobs (S3=A); this is not whole-app multi-session S3. A separate model auditor explores the existing knowledge graph to correct proposition identity/duplicate links before ledger merge (S3*=C), but it does not see the raw source or fact-check the lesson. Current web research and accumulated learner knowledge do not constitute an external prospective capability-adaptation cycle (S4=—); learner goal selection and static teaching prompts do not resolve a constitutional identity dispute (S5=—). BYOK core mode; frozen source only, no live provider tests.
780|apodex-agentharness|One first-party react_base research S1 owns search/fetch/code decisions; fixed-budget observers and out-of-loop benchmark judges constrain or evaluate the solver without closing a metasystem.
781|harness-zero|Harness-Zero's training-time student agent executes environment tasks through model/tool feedback (S1=A), while a separate harnessing teacher autonomously PASSes or REPLACEs unexecuted candidate actions for the current trial (S3=A). This vertical supervision does not resolve interference among independent S1 units (S2=—), and the same mandatory inline review is not a separate complementary audit (S3*=—). Offline harness-bank evolution, reward-tagged SFT and later minimal-harness deployment do not close a prospective adaptation loop inside the reviewed trial (S4=—); configuration, prompts and training confirmation do not close identity-level policy (S5=—). Pinned source-only assessment; no live model-quality run.
784|crewctl|CrewCtl runs specialist coding agents under an autonomous operator that plans, delegates, interprets task outcomes and makes current continue/complete decisions (S1=A, S3=A). Optional concurrent task worktrees specifically isolate distinct worker teams against shared-file/event interference, but do not add autonomous cross-team conflict arbitration (S2=C). The packaged direct-inspection reviewer and verdict-to-operator return construct complementary audit, while reviewer independence/read-only enforcement remains incomplete (S3*=C). Project context updates, provider fallback, scheduled tasks and human risk consent do not close future-oriented adaptation or identity-level policy (S4=—, S5=—). Source-pinned evaluation; no live provider-quality test.
786|savant-code|Savant-Code's first-party model/tool coding agent performs repository tasks (S1=A) under a model-led current Orchestrator for work delegation and rework (S3=A). Optional registered per-FID worktrees isolate agent task lanes and explicitly refuse stale/conflicting merges without autonomous inter-team conflict adjudication (S2=C). Separate Verifier and read-only Adversary agents challenge raw change/test evidence and return contradictory verdicts into current control (S3*=A in the supported review mode). Cross-session failure patterns generate quarantined skill proposals, and a legitimate local human operator must inspect/approve trust before later agents load the new capability (S4=P). Static ECHO laws, risk approvals and skill promotion do not establish identity-level policy authority (S5=—). Source-only assessment; Bun tests and live provider runs not performed.
785|deepresearch-agent-harness|DeepResearch Agent Harness ships model-backed research planning, retrieval and report/repair choices in persisted first-party runs (S1=A). Optional parallel worker isolation, dependency checks and deterministic ordered state merge attenuate shared-work interference (S2=C), without an autonomous coordinator. Fixed run budgets and lifecycle controls do not close whole-current S3 (—); mandatory contract verification is not complementary independent audit (S3*=—). Optional trace-backed Proposer/Critic review and staged promotion can improve later skill selection only with explicit human approval (S4=C(P), not autonomous promotion); no identity-level S5 closure (—). Pinned source inspection, no live model tests.
787|hrn|A native API coding loop combines issue/worktree collision constructors and a complementary blinded-diff judge with operator-adopted model routing from comparative evidence.
788|dtadpole-heddle|Heddle couples a native model/tool loop with an optional model-led Team mode: task-claim CAS and dependency feedback address teammate collisions (S2=A), while the Lead sees whole-team commitments and can reassign or interrupt current work (S3=A). Development verifiers are outside the installed audit boundary; no shipped S3*/S4/S5 closure is established.
791|setup-ai-core|setup-ai-core directly invokes a configured Claude/Codex/agy coding-agent CLI to inspect source and synthesize a checked repository map, then commits and delivers that map into future agent context (narrow S1=A). The coordinator and worker phase skills, board/locks/watchdog, different-model review and owner approvals rely on separately initiated external host actors or deterministic gates; no first-party organizational S2/S3/S3*/S4/S5 closure is established at the declared boundary. Static pinned-source evidence; no live model test.
792|agentflow-venkatapgummadi|AgentFlow's opt-in LLM intent parser selects structured API operations and dependencies, which a deterministic planner and connector executor turn into a bounded workflow (narrow S1=A; default rule/mock mode is not agentic). DAG scheduling, endpoint/budget routing, routine result validation, local adaptive scoring and external host configuration do not establish first-party S2/S3/S3*/S4/S5 closure. Pinned source only; several executor branches are placeholders, no live model or enterprise API test.
