892|kheish|Kheish runs durable model/tool/observation sessions (S1=A); its daemon rejects duplicate or dependency-invalid project starts (S2=C), while a configured model coordinator inspects task/child state and revises current assignments (S3=A). Logs, routine reviews, learning and approvals do not establish S3*/S4/S5.
893|ralphify|Ralphify repeats a configured model-agent CLI with fresh prompts fed by current project/test commands (S1=A). Its multi-run registry, timeout/stop controls and user-edited loop definitions support operations but provide no cross-run S2 coordination or independent S3/S3*/S4/S5 governance.
894|wanman|wanman's local matrix delegates model/tool work to isolated agents (S1=A), rejects overlapping declared task/capsule scopes through fixed collision gates (S2=C), and equips a model CEO to monitor and revise current project commitments (S3=A). CEO/CTO routine reviews are not independent S3*; issue/roadmap mining is not a distinct prospective S4; human-defined mission and approvals do not establish S5.
895|feather|Feather production teams let a model parent partition shared-file writing and revise child commitments through status, follow-up and stop tools (S1/S2/S3=A). Separate Train/Eval graders and training are outside the installed team; no independent S3*, prospective S4 or ultimate-policy S5 loop is supplied.
896|s1code|S1Code's native Rust loop makes model-driven repository edits with revision-bound approvals and verification (S1=A); deterministic selection, evidence storage and optional external Codex delegation do not establish S2/S3/S3*/S4/S5 ownership.
897|zleap-agent|Zleap model workspaces act on scoped tools (S1=A); stale-history locks and handoff replay/cycle guards supply S2=C. A task-portfolio ledger and update tools expose S3=C, with operator history/pause/binding changes returned to the worker in a supported P mode. Memory/dream processing is not independent S3* or prospective S4; persona/approvals are not S5.
898|djcode|DJcode's model-backed Operator and mutating specialists run repository-facing tools (S1=A). A separately instantiated read-only Reviewer can directly inspect code and return corrective findings to the Operator (S3*=A). Parallel scheduling and shared context do not close interference-specific S2 or live S3 control; persistence and configured roles do not establish S4/S5.
899|agent-from-scratch|Agent From Scratch's parent model edits and tests code (S1=A). A built-in read-only reviewer runs in a separate model/context/tool loop and returns source-grounded defect findings to the parent (S3*=A). One mutating parent and read-only children do not establish S2/S3; memory and offline benchmarks do not close S4, and configured permissions do not establish S5.
900|novacode|NovaCode's Team Lead revises live assignments and returns implementation-plan decisions to independent coding workers (S3=A); worktree isolation supplies collision-specific S2 construction, and the packaged no-context fork review audits raw code (S3*=A). Memory, prompts and permissions do not close S4/S5.
901|codeforge|CodeForge's single coding model revises files and retries sandboxed execution from tool/error/denial feedback (S1=A). SQLite resumable per-action approval and syntax/import gates constrain that loop; fixed budgets, ordinary validation, package installation and static prompts do not establish S2-S5.
902|codinggirl|CodingGirl's Desktop/Python-backed model chooses repository tools and receives results (S1=A), unlike its scaffold run command and deterministic replacement orchestrator. Read-only research delegation, generic parallel ordering, normal event replay and patch predicates do not supply distinct S2-S5 closure.
